{"id": "y0985", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 18, "onset_chunk": 18, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, let's figure this out. So we have a metal reacting with H Cl to produce hydrogen gas at ST P. We're given that 0.0 623 gra ms of metal produces 28.3 mill iliters of hydrogen. First thing, at ST P, one mole of any gas is 2 2.4 liters, or 2 2,40 0 milliliters. So 2 8.3 mill iliters of hydrogen is going to be 2 8.3 divided by 2 2,4 00 mo les. Let me calculate that quickly. That comes out to about 0. 001 26 moles of hydrogen. Now, assuming the reaction is like M plus 2 HCl gives MC l2 plus H2, so one mole of metal react s with two moles of HCl to produce one mole of hydrogen. So if 0.0 0 126 mo les of H2 came from 0. 0 62 3 grams of metal, then for one mole of HCl, which", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
