{"id": "y1049", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 12, "onset_chunk": 11, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "A closed dry er set to run for 30 minutes is an example of open-loop control. In this case, the system operates based solely on a predetermined time setting without continuously monitoring the actual drying conditions or making adjustment s in response to them. So the correct answer is A. Open- loop.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 19, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "y1057", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 22, "onset_chunk": 22, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so let's go through this step by step. First, if A is finite and B is a subset of A, then B can only contain elements that are already in A. Since there are finitely many elements in A, any subset, including B, must also be finite. So statement 1 is true. Now, for regular languages, regularity is closed under subsets. That means if A is regular, then every subset of it, like B, must also be regular. So statement 2 is also true. However, context free language s are not necessarily closed under subsets. There exist context-free language s where some subset is not context-free. For example, consider the language of all palindrom es over an alphabet; it is context-free, but its subset consisting of even-length palin dromes might not be. So statement 3 is false. Therefore, statements 1 and 2 must always be true. The correct answer is D: 1 and 2 only.", "eot_seen": true, "n_ans_chunks": 46, "carrier_ended": null}
{"id": "y1065", "pool": "know-mmlu", "n_q_chunks": 37, "onset_chunk": 37, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, so let's break this down. We have two processor s, M5 and M7, both using the same instruction set. But they have different pipeline structures and clock speeds. M5 has a 5-stage pipe line with a 10-nanose cond clock cycle, while M 7 has a 7- stage pipeline but a faster 7.5- nanosecond clock. So let’s look at each statement one by one. First, about maximum throughput. Throughput is generally better when the clock cycle time is shorter because more instructions can be processed per second. Even though M7 has more stages, its clock is faster. So yes, M7 likely has better maximum through put. Next, the lat ency of a single instruction. In a pipelined processor, the latency for an instruction is roughly equal to the number of pipeline stages times the clock cycle. For M5, it would be about 5 * 10 = 50 nanoseconds. For M 7, it would be 7 * 7. 5 = about 52. 5 nano seconds. So actually,", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
